# /etc/qmcp/consent-policy — operator-owned consent gate (Stage I-6: EMPTY).
#
# This file selects which (service, action) pairs require live operator consent
# before the dom0 wrapper performs the privileged operation. One rule per line:
#
#     service:action
#
# where `action` may be '*' to gate every action of that service. Lines
# starting with '#' and blank lines are ignored. The file is re-read on every
# gated call, so edits take effect with NO daemon restart.
#
# ── Stage I-6 ships this file EMPTY (all lines below are comments) ────────────
# An empty gate means consent_required() returns False for every call, the
# consent daemon is never contacted, and behaviour is byte-identical to the
# pre-I-6 tree (invariance). The mechanism is installed but INERT. Turning it on
# is Stage I-7: uncomment the rules you want (or your own), and each matching
# call will pop the operator consent dialog.
#
# FAIL-CLOSED: if this file is MISSING or contains ANY unparseable non-comment
# line, the helper falls back to gating the destructive-by-default set —
# LifecycleAIManaged:remove/kill and Attach/DetachDeviceAIManaged — rather than
# gating nothing. So a corrupt policy denies more, never less.
#
# Only the four @adminvm-wrapper surfaces below are gateable here; firewall
# write and exec/copy are policy-scoped surfaces (not wrappers) and cannot be
# gated through this file.
#
# ── The intended Stage I-7 default (uncomment to enable) ─────────────────────
# qmcp.LifecycleAIManaged:remove
# qmcp.LifecycleAIManaged:kill
# qmcp.AttachDeviceAIManaged:attach
# qmcp.DetachDeviceAIManaged:detach
#
# ── Further examples (uncomment / adapt as you like) ─────────────────────────
# Gate EVERY lifecycle action (start/shutdown/kill/pause/unpause/remove):
# qmcp.LifecycleAIManaged:*
#
# Gate every property write and every clone:
# qmcp.SetPropertyAIManaged:set-property
# qmcp.CloneAIManagedQube:clone
